From b61a9a61ab34a88b07d4c2839b76b08e8da2b23f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:44:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/27] browser: import HAR 1.2 captures, redacted on the way
 in
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

HAR support was write-only: HarRecorder emitted captures, but nothing could read
one back, so a capture taken outside Tallow had no way into the pipeline.

The blocker was real, not cosmetic: every HAR struct in network.rs derived
Serialize only, so the shapes the recorder produces could not be parsed. They now
derive Deserialize, with defaults for what a real capture omits (timings.ssl,
pages, sizes) and aliases for the two spellings our own rename_all gets wrong
(serverIPAddress, redirectURL). One shape — not a parallel import-side copy that
would drift from the recorder's.

har_import refuses rather than half-succeeds: a parse error, a version that is not
1.2, or a structurally valid capture carrying no entries (an imported empty
capture is indistinguishable, later, from "we looked and found nothing").
Redaction is not optional and not bypassable: imported URLs, header values and
body text pass through the same browser::privacy scanner a live capture obeys, so
"hand me a HAR file" is not a route around the rules the live path enforces.

Verified: cargo check --lib --bins RC=0; browser::har_import 7 passed / 0 failed;
browser::network 63 passed / 0 failed — the new derives do not disturb the
recorder, and one test reads the committed fixture so it cannot silently drift.
